## Project Overview

      Completed a hands-on cybersecurity lab focused on identifying and analyzing indicators of malicious activity on Windows and Linux systems. The lab provided practical experience monitoring system resource utilization, using process analysis tools to inspect running processes, and configuring Active Directory auditing to detect unauthorized privilege escalation. The exercises aligned with CompTIA CySA+ (CS0-003) Exam Objective 1.2: analyzing indicators of potentially malicious activity (network-related, host-related, and application-related).

## Lab Environment

      ACIDC01: Windows Server 2022 Domain Controller
      ACIDM01: Windows Server 2022 Domain Member Server
      ACIALMA: Alma Linux Standalone Workstation

## Key Accomplishments
      1. Monitoring Resource Utilization on a Windows Device
      
      Used built-in and advanced Windows tools to monitor CPU, memory, disk, and network activity and to recognize behavior that could indicate an attack.
      
      Reviewed CPU, memory, and Ethernet performance data in Task Manager
      Used Resource Monitor to examine per-process activity across the CPU, Memory, Disk, and Network tabs
      Used Sysinternals Process Explorer to view the full process tree, including detailed CPU, memory, I/O, and GPU metrics
      Practiced terminating a process from Process Explorer, as would be done to contain a suspicious process
      
      This exercise showed how sudden spikes in resource use can point to malware, denial-of-service activity, or unauthorized workloads such as cryptocurrency miners. It also showed how much more detail Process Explorer provides than the native Task Manager.
      
      2. Monitoring Resource Utilization on a Linux Device
      
      Connected from a Windows server to the Alma Linux workstation over SSH and used command-line tools to investigate running processes.
      
      Used top to view real-time process activity and limit the number of iterations
      Filtered processes by user account with top -u to isolate activity tied to a specific account
      Installed the EPEL repository and the htop utility using dnf
      Used htop and its user filter to review color-coded processes on the system
      
      This reinforced how filtering process output by user can help uncover unexpected or malicious applications running on a Linux host.
      
      3. Enabling User Privilege Monitoring
      
      Configured auditing in Active Directory so that changes to privileged group membership would be recorded.
      
      Opened Group Policy Management on the domain controller and edited the Default Domain Policy
      Navigated to Advanced Audit Policy Configuration and enabled Audit Security Group Management for both Success and Failure events
      Forced the policy to apply immediately with gpupdate /force
      
      This showed how audit policy settings determine what security-relevant activity gets logged. In a production environment, I would scope this to a dedicated Group Policy rather than the Default Domain Policy.
      
      4. Generating Auditing Logs
      
      Created activity that the new audit policy should capture.
      
      Created a new domain user account in the Marketing OU using Active Directory Users and Computers
      Added the new account to the Domain Admins group, simulating an unauthorized privilege escalation
      
      This demonstrated how an attacker who has compromised an account might create an obscure account, grant it elevated privileges, and use it to maintain access.
      
      5. Detecting an Unauthorized Privilege Escalation
      
      Used Event Viewer on the domain controller to find and analyze the resulting security events.
      
      Located Event ID 4728, showing a member added to a security-enabled global group, and identified which account made the change and which account was added to Domain Admins
      Located Event ID 4737, showing that a security-enabled group was modified
      Reviewed the Details tab of each event to understand the data the audit policy captured
      
      These events indicate that the account making the changes may have been compromised, or that privileges were changed without authorization.

